=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch_dist_2_1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The OSPF area assignment for the uplink interface `eth0` (`172.16.0.9/31`) on `switch_dist_2_1` (FRR `router ospf` stanza).

**Mechanism:** OSPF requires that both ends of a point-to-point link declare the same area. `router_core_2` places its side of the `172.16.0.8/31` link into **area 0.0.0.1** (`network 172.16.0.8/31 area 1`). `switch_dist_2_1` incorrectly places its side into **area 0.0.0.0** (`network 172.16.0.8/31 area 0`). Because the Hello packets carry different area IDs, the two routers never pass the area-ID check and the adjacency never advances past `Init` — neither a 2-Way nor a Full state is reached. With zero OSPF neighbors, `switch_dist_2_1` only retains its three directly connected routes and has no path to the server farm (`10.200.0.0/24`) or pod 1 (`10.1.1.0/24`). Traffic from `host_2_1_1_1` reaches the gateway `10.2.1.1` but is immediately returned with ICMP `Destination Net Unreachable`.

**Localization:** `switch_dist_2_1`, FRR OSPF process, `network 172.16.0.8/31 area 0` statement. The physical link (`eth0` ↔ `router_core_2:eth2`) is confirmed UP; only the control-plane area assignment is wrong.

**Root cause:** A change-window misconfiguration placed `switch_dist_2_1`'s uplink network (`172.16.0.8/31`) in OSPF area 0 instead of area 1, causing a permanent area-ID mismatch with `router_core_2` and preventing any OSPF adjacency from forming.

**Fix:** On `switch_dist_2_1`, in `vtysh`, replace `no network 172.16.0.8/31 area 0` with `network 172.16.0.8/31 area 1` under `router ospf`. This aligns both ends of the link in area 0.0.0.1, restoring the OSPF adjacency and re-propagating all routes to `host_2_1_1_1`.
=== END ===